description.narrative:
After the promulgation of the new Constitution it is now a momentous time in the history of Kenya to shape our identity as an emerging democracy. On one hand, there are great opportunities for active citizen leadership, good governance, and promotion of human rights; while on the other, as a society in transition, the threats of ethnic intolerance and tribal affiliations are real. Jesuit Hakimani Centre is proposing to script, produce, and if possible broadcast a TV series to be entitled The Librarian aimed at sensitizing grassroots communities and leaders at the County level on understanding and embracing social diversities as enshrined in the new Constitution. This is only possible if people develop constructive debates. The drama will include:
o	Scripting and production ,
o	Screening at County level for public debate, 
o	TV broadcast (if and when possible)

The project is expected to directly reach 3000 – 5000 grassroots community leaders at the end of the implementation period (just before the general elections are held in March 2013. However, a large audience is expected to be reached through a TV broadcast (if budget allows). 
The Librarian Series will emphasize: 
•	The social impact of diversity;
•	Strategies of social dialogue: self-identity, authority, inter-subjective relationships and ‘the other’;
•	Social conflict analysis strategies;
•	Upholding national values as written in the new Constitution
